  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:23:24.702Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nSubstituted filing does not apply to employees with two employers during the year. According to Section 51 of the NIRC, individuals deriving compensation concurrently from two or more employers must file an income tax return.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **NIRC Section 51**: This section states that individuals deriving compensation from two or more employers at any time during the taxable year are required to file an income tax return.\n\n### Interpretation\nSubstituted filing allows employees receiving purely compensation income from a single employer, where the tax has been correctly withheld, to avoid filing an annual income tax return. However, this provision does not extend to individuals with multiple employers, as they are required to file their returns to accurately report their total income and tax liabilities.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor employees with two employers, it is essential to file an income tax return to ensure compliance with tax regulations. This requirement helps the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR) assess the total income earned and the corresponding tax due, preventing potential issues related to underreporting income or tax evasion.",
